				"unit": "%"
			},
			"title": "Scheduled %",
			"description": "Percentage of searches running on schedule"
		},
		"viz_ts_box_kpi": {
			"type": "splunk.rectangle",
			"options": {
				"fillColor": "rgba(255,255,255,0.78)",
				"strokeColor": "rgba(0,0,0,0.08)",
				"rx": 10
			}
		},
		"viz_ts_box_cache": {
			"type": "splunk.rectangle",
			"options": {
				"fillColor": "rgba(255,255,255,0.78)",
				"strokeColor": "rgba(0,0,0,0.08)",
				"rx": 10
			}
		},
		"viz_ts_cache_table": {
			"type": "splunk.table",
			"dataSources": {
				"primary": "ds_cache_summary"
			},
			"options": {
				"count": 20
			},
			"title": "Cache Hit Rate by Dashboard",
			"description": "Per-dashboard cache efficiency — higher hit rate means fewer redundant searches"
		},
		"viz_ts_info": {
			"type": "splunk.markdown",
			"options": {
				"markdown": "### How to Read This Report\n\n---\n\n**CACHE HIT** — Dashboard reused a pre-computed scheduler artifact (fast, no new search dispatched)\n\n**CACHE MISS** — Dashboard dispatched a fresh search because no cached artifact was found\n\n---\n\n**Why would a search miss?**\n- The saved search is **not scheduled** (`NOT SCHEDULED`)\n- The scheduler artifact **expired** (check Age vs schedule interval)\n- The search was an **inline search** (`INLINE`)\n\n**How to fix misses:** Go to **Settings > Searches, Reports, and Alerts**, find the search, click **Edit > Edit Schedule**, enable scheduling and set the cron expression.\n\n---\n\n### How Saved Search Caching Works\n\nAll dashboards use `ds.savedSearch` data sources that reference scheduled saved searches. When a dashboard loads:\n\n1. **Scheduled + `ds.savedSearch` (`ref`)** — Splunk loads the latest cached result from the scheduler. No new search runs. This is what produces a **CACHE HIT**.\n2. **Non-scheduled + `ds.savedSearch` (`ref`)** — Splunk runs a fresh search since no cached artifact exists. This is a **CACHE MISS**.\n3. **`ds.search` with `| savedsearch`** — Always runs a new search regardless of schedule. Use only when tokens are needed.\n\nConcurrency limits apply: if a dashboard has more `ds.savedSearch` data sources than the concurrency limit allows, excess searches will not execute. Scheduling all searches ensures cached artifacts are always ready.\n\nFor full details, see the Splunk documentation: [Call saved searches — Dashboard Studio](https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-cloud-platform/create-dashboards-and-reports/dashboard-studio/10.3.2512/use-data-sources/call-saved-searches)",
				"backgroundColor": "#ffffff"
			}
		},
		"viz_ts_box_slow": {
			"type": "splunk.rectangle",
			"options": {
				"fillColor": "rgba(255,255,255,0.78)",
				"strokeColor": "rgba(0,0,0,0.08)",
